--- line 184104 ---
The polynomial in question is \(X^{3} - 2\) considered as an element of \(\mathbb{Q}[X]\).  
A degree‑3 polynomial over a field is reducible iff it has a linear factor, i.e. a root in the field.  
If a rational number \(\frac{p}{q}\) (in lowest terms) were a root, the rational root theorem forces \(p\mid 2\) and \(q\mid 1\); hence the only possible rational roots are \(\pm1,\pm2\). Direct substitution shows that none of these values satisfy \((\pm1)^{3}-2= -1\) or \((\pm2)^{3}-2=6\). Therefore the polynomial has no rational root, and consequently no linear factor over \(\mathbb{Q}\).  

Since a degree‑3 polynomial without a linear factor cannot factor into a product of lower‑degree non‑constant polynomials, it is irreducible in \(\mathbb{Q}[X]\). Hence the statement “\(X^{3} - 2\) is irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}\)” is mathematically correct.
</justification>
